## STEP 2 (designed) -- the CLI surface, enumerated read-only before anything was typed

`tools/tb qmark` types `PREFIX ?` with no carriage return and clears the line with Ctrl-U; its
self-test proves nothing was executed (`self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U then CR executed
nothing`). Files: `work/s0-qmark-config.log`, `-config2.log`, `-config3.log`, `-intf.log`,
`work/s11-qmark-alarmstartup.log`, `work/s12-qmark-alarmstartup2.log`.

Config mode:

    rmon alarm 1 ?            ->  WORD  variable for setting alarm(etherStatsEntry.n.n)
    rmon event 1 ?            ->  description | log | owner | trap | <cr>
    rmon event 1 trap ?       ->  WORD  event community
    snmp-server community ?   ->  WORD  Community name
    snmp-server community testing123 ?  ->  <1-99> | ro | rw | view | <cr>
    snmp-server host ?        ->  A.B.C.D | X:X::X:X          (NO port option at any level)
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 ?        -> WORD | informs | traps | version | vrf
    snmp-server host 10.38.22.33 version ? -> 1 | 2c | 3

The full alarm grammar, probed token by token:

    rmon alarm 1 etherStatsEntry.5.1 ?                                  -> interval
    ... interval 10 ?                                                   -> absolute | delta
    ... interval 10 delta ?                                             -> rising-threshold
    ... delta rising-threshold ?                                        -> <1-2147483647>
    ... rising-threshold 10 ?                                           -> event
    ... event 1 ?                                                       -> falling-threshold
    ... falling-threshold ?                                             -> <1-2147483647>
    ... falling-threshold 1 event 2 ?                                   -> alarmstartup | owner | <cr>
    ... falling-threshold 1 event 12 alarmstartup ?                     -> <1-3>  Alarm StartUp value

Interface mode (`interface port1.0.10`):

    rmon collection ?           ->  history | stats
    rmon collection stats ?     ->  <1-65535>  Stat Index
    rmon collection stats 1 ?   ->  owner | <cr>
    rmon collection history ?   ->  <1-65535>  history Index
    rmon collection history 1 ? ->  buckets (default 50) | interval (default 1800) | owner | <cr>
    snmp trap ?                 ->  link-status | mac-change

`snmp-server enable trap ?` offers 32 groups. `rmon` is one of them (the list is in
`work/s0-qmark-config.log`; `nsm` and `hw-monitoring` are there too, which the next two cases
need). **STEP 2 PASS** -- every token this case sends was read off the device first.

### 🔥 FINDING (CLI) -- an out-of-range threshold is reported as `% Unrecognized command`, pointing at the NEXT keyword

    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 12 etherStatsEntry.6.11 interval 10 delta rising-threshold 1
                    event 11 falling-threshold 0 event 12 alarmstartup ?
    % Unrecognized command

Re-probing the same line with `falling-threshold 1` instead of `0` gives
`<1-3>  Alarm StartUp value` -- so `alarmstartup` is a perfectly good keyword and the real
error is the `0`, eleven tokens earlier. Both thresholds are `<1-2147483647>`.

Two consequences, both worth a developer's eye:

1. **The diagnostic points at the wrong token.** A number outside its range silently makes the
   parser abandon the rule, and the `^` lands on whatever word follows. This is exactly the shape
   of error that burns a bench session: the engineer deletes `alarmstartup`, the line still
   fails, and the actual offender is never suspected.
2. **`alarmFallingThreshold = 0` cannot be configured at all.** RFC 2819 places no such floor on
   it, and for a `delta` alarm on a packet counter "the rate fell to zero" is the single most
   natural falling condition there is. On this build the lowest expressible falling threshold is
   1, so "no traffic at all" and "one packet" cannot be distinguished.

