## STEP 5 (designed) -- what the over-count is, measured in an idle window

`work/s8-step1d-idle.log`. Counters read, then a **30 s capture of everything on eth3 with no
traffic injected at all**, then the counters read again:

| column | before | after | delta |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| etherStatsOctets | 219072 | 219382 | 310 |
| etherStatsPkts | 2535 | 2538 | **3** |
| Pkts64Octets | 126 | 146 | 20 |
| Pkts65to127Octets | 2810 | 2844 | 34 |
| Pkts128to255Octets | 13 | 15 | 2 |
| **bucket total** | | | **56** |

The capture over the same window holds **50 frames**, of which only 2 ARP frames were inbound to
the DUT. (`tools/vcount.py` itemises ARP/IPv4 only -- 5 of the 50; the other 45 are the DUT's own
1 Hz loop-protection LDFs, RSTP BPDUs and LLDP, all EGRESS, at the ~1.5 frames/s that 45 frames
in 30 s implies.)

### 🔥 FINDING (product) -- the RMON per-frame-size counters count BOTH directions; etherStatsPkts counts ingress only

In a window where **3** frames entered port1.0.10, the frame-size buckets counted **56**, and the
wire in that same window carried **50** frames nearly all of which were transmitted BY the DUT.
The same excess appears in every leg and always matches the DUT's own egress over the window
(42 and 27 over bursts of a few seconds, 53 over 30 idle seconds).

RFC 2819 defines `etherStatsPkts` and every one of `etherStatsPkts64Octets` ..
`etherStatsPkts1024to1518Octets` identically, as *"the total number of packets (including bad
packets) received"*. The size buckets are meant to be a **partition of etherStatsPkts**. Here
their sum consistently **exceeds** it, so a manager that charts the size distribution against the
packet count on this platform gets percentages over 100%. The first read after creating the
collection showed the same split from the other side: `etherStatsPkts = 0` and
`etherStatsOctets = 0` while `Pkts64 = 10` and `Pkts65to127 = 21` were already non-zero.

**This is a product finding for a developer, not a bench problem.** It does not change the case's
verdict: every graded claim in this log rests on `etherStatsPkts`, `etherStatsOctets`,
`etherHistoryPkts` and `etherHistoryOctets`, all four of which were measured exact.

