### ⚠️ OBSERVATION -- the two numbers on one line of `show rmon statistics` have different epochs

At the moment the collection was created, `show rmon statistics` printed
`input packets 0, bytes 394600`. `input packets` starts at zero with the collection (it tracks
etherStatsPkts); `bytes` does not -- it carries the interface's pre-existing lifetime octet
count. Checked again later: `input packets 3539, bytes 700110` against etherStatsOctets of
~305,400 -- a difference of ~394,700, i.e. the same offset. So the two figures printed side by
side are measured from different starting points, which reads as a corrupt counter until you
work out why. Cosmetic; recorded so the next tester does not chase it.

## STEP 7 (designed) -- `rmon event` + `rmon alarm`, and a REAL trap on the wire

    awplus(config)# rmon event 11 log trap testing123 description ck44308rise owner ck44308
    awplus(config)# rmon event 12 log trap testing123 description ck44308fall owner ck44308
    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 11 etherStatsEntry.5.11 interval 10 delta rising-threshold 100
                    event 11 falling-threshold 10 event 12 alarmstartup 3 owner ck44308
    awplus(config)# rmon alarm 12 etherStatsEntry.6.11 interval 10 delta rising-threshold 1
                    event 11 falling-threshold 1 event 12 alarmstartup 1 owner ck44308

- **alarm 11 is the measurement**: etherStatsPkts on port1.0.10, delta over 10 s, rising at 100.
- **alarm 12 is the control**: identical in every respect except that it watches
  **etherStatsBroadcastPkts**, which measured 0 through every earlier leg, and its startup type is
  1 (rising only) so it cannot fire a startup falling alarm. It MUST stay silent while alarm 11
  fires -- and it must still be able to fire when its own condition is finally met, which is why
  the sequence ends with 50 broadcast frames.

`show rmon alarm` reads both back correctly (`work/s13-step3-alarms.log`), with
`alarm Variable = etherStatsPkts.11` and `etherStatsBroadcastPkts.11`, `alarm Type is Delta`,
and the thresholds, events, startup types and owners as configured. `show rmon event` shows both
events as `Event type Log & Trap`, `Event community name testing123`.

