          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.4.11 = INTEGER: 2                                alarmSampleType = deltaValue
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.5.11 = INTEGER: 553                              alarmValue
          1.3.6.1.2.1.16.3.1.1.7.11 = INTEGER: 100                              alarmRisingThreshold
    #2   10:13:57.446 ...  1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.0.2   <- fallingAlarm
          alarmIndex 11, alarmVariable etherStatsPkts.11, sampleType 2, alarmValue 0, alarmFallingThreshold 10
    #3   10:14:18.222 ...  1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.0.1   <- risingAlarm
          alarmIndex 12, alarmVariable .1.3.6.1.2.1.16.1.1.1.6.11 (etherStatsBroadcastPkts.11),
          sampleType 2, alarmValue 50, alarmRisingThreshold 1
    #4   10:14:28.222 ...  1.3.6.1.6.3.1.1.4.1.0 = OID: 1.3.6.1.2.1.16.0.2   <- fallingAlarm
          alarmIndex 12, alarmVariable etherStatsBroadcastPkts.11, sampleType 2, alarmValue 0,
          alarmFallingThreshold 1
    SUMMARY 4 SNMP message(s): 0 ENCRYPTED, 4 PLAINTEXT

**What that pairs up to:**

| window | what was sent | what arrived on udp/162 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 10:13:00-10:13:25 | nothing | **nothing** (control) |
| 10:13:33-10:13:40 | 1000 unicast frames | risingAlarm, **alarm 11**, value **553** vs threshold 100 |
| 10:13:40-10:14:11 | nothing | fallingAlarm, alarm 11, value 0 vs threshold 10 |
| the whole unicast burst | 1000 unicast, 0 broadcast | **alarm 12 silent throughout** (control) |
| 10:14:11 | **50** broadcast frames | risingAlarm, **alarm 12**, value **exactly 50** vs threshold 1 |
| 10:14:28 onward | nothing | fallingAlarm, alarm 12, value 0; then nothing to 10:15:00 |

The 553 is the part of the 1000-frame burst that fell inside the 10-second sample the alarm was
on when it crossed; the rest arrived in the following sample, which is why the next event is the
fall back to 0 rather than a second rise. The control alarm's value is **exactly the number of
frames sent**, 50, which is as direct as this measurement gets. **STEP 7 PASS.**

